Title:
NewWorldInterface

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- NewWorldInterface is a Windows usermode “kernel interface” client that talks to a companion kernel driver using named shared-memory sections and kernel events.  
- It provides an interactive command loop for inspecting and manipulating a target process’s VAD tree, performing cross-process virtual memory operations, and reading/writing physical memory through the driver.  
- A key design point is build-agnostic operation: it parses `ntoskrnl.exe`, locates/downloads the matching PDB from Microsoft Symbol Server, and resolves required kernel offsets/RVAs at runtime via DbgHelp rather than hardcoding offsets.  
- The tool exposes higher-risk internals operations like VAD node insert/delete, mapping section Control Areas into other processes, and relinking PTEs to point at chosen physical pages (with an “unlink” rollback event).  
- It’s primarily useful for Windows internals research, red team tradecraft prototyping, reverse engineering, and memory forensics experimentation where kernel structure access is required.  
- Interesting because it combines runtime symbol resolution with a driver-backed IPC workflow to enable iterative manipulation of paging/VAD structures across Windows 10/11 builds.

Technical Focus:
- Runtime kernel symbol/offset resolution (PDB, DbgHelp: `SymInitializeW`, `SymLoadModuleExW`)
- IPC via named shared memory sections and kernel events (driver ↔ usermode)
- VAD tree walking/indexing and VAD node insertion/deletion
- PTE manipulation / virtual-to-physical page relinking and rollback (“unlink”)
- Cross-process memory read/write and protection changes
- Physical memory access and pattern scanning

Use Cases:
- VAD-based injection and memory region manipulation research
- Cross-process section/view mapping experiments (Control Area sharing)
- Investigating paging structures (PTEs) and testing page relinking concepts
- Driver-assisted process dumping (minidump) under constrained protections/handles
- Memory forensics workflows requiring kernel-accurate VAD/PTE visibility

Keywords:
Windows kernel, ntoskrnl.exe, PDB, Microsoft Symbol Server, DbgHelp, SymInitializeW, SymLoadModuleExW, VAD tree, MMVAD, Control Area, section object, PTE, paging structures, physical memory read/write, shared memory section, kernel events, IPC, cross-process memory, minidump, Windows internals